/**
\defgroup distrib_vonmises Von Mises
\brief Continuous circular distribution parametrized by mean direction and circular standard deviation, with concentration map, log-density derivatives to order 6, closed expected information, and cumulative function.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/vonmises.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::vonmises_distrib "vonmises_distrib", this module evaluates the von Mises log-density, its parameter derivatives to order \f$6\f$, mixed observation-parameter derivatives, closed expected Fisher information with parameter derivatives to order \f$4\f$, cumulative and complementary cumulative distribution functions with their logarithms, regularity metadata, sample starting values, and the circular standard deviation to concentration map (\ref laplace::distrib::vonmises_kappa_from_s "vonmises_kappa_from_s()", \ref laplace::distrib::vonmises_kappa_from_s_dk "vonmises_kappa_from_s_dk()", and \ref laplace::distrib::vonmises_kappa_from_s_upto "vonmises_kappa_from_s_upto()"). Every evaluator except `start_value()` is marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host CPUs and Kokkos device kernels (\ref distrib_core "distrib_core"). Quantiles, random draws, and parameter derivatives of \f$\log F\f$ and \f$\log(1 - F)\f$ use \ref distrib_generic "the generic routes". Against mpmath (The mpmath development team, 2023) references, log-density tensors stay within \f$5.16\f$ scaled ulp for \f$s \le 4.5\f$ (\f$0.81\f$ above \f$4.5\f$), the concentration map stays within \f$25.7\f$ ulp on \f$s \in [10^{-8}, 4.5]\f$, and the cumulative function stays within \f$0.24\f$ of its test tolerances.

## Classic form and parametrization

On the circle, with observation \f$y\f$, mean direction \f$\mu \in \mathbb{R}\f$, concentration \f$\kappa > 0\f$, and wrapped difference \f$z = y - \mu\f$ wrapped to \f$(-\pi, \pi]\f$, the classic von Mises density (Mardia and Jupp, 2000) is \f$f_{\mathrm{classic}}(y; \mu, \kappa) = \exp(\kappa \cos z) / (2\pi I_0(\kappa))\f$, where \f$I_\nu\f$ is the modified Bessel function of the first kind of order \f$\nu\f$ (DLMF §10.40). The mean resultant length is \f$\rho = A(\kappa) = I_1(\kappa)/I_0(\kappa)\f$ (Mardia and Jupp, 2000). Defining the circular standard deviation \f$s = \sqrt{-2\log\rho} > 0\f$, so that \f$\rho = e^{-s^2/2}\f$ and \f$\kappa = \kappa(s) = A^{-1}(e^{-s^2/2})\f$, makes \f$s\f$ the standard deviation of the wrapped normal distribution with the same mean resultant length (Mardia and Jupp, 2000); \f$s\f$ is positive on \f$(0, \infty)\f$ (covering the real line under a logarithmic link) and \f$s^2 \approx 1/\kappa\f$ when the law is concentrated. Under this substitution, the implemented density is
\f[
f(y; \mu, s) = \frac{\exp\bigl(\kappa(s)\cos z\bigr)}{2\pi I_0\bigl(\kappa(s)\bigr)},
\f]
and the implemented log-density is
\f[
\ell(\mu, s; y) = \kappa(s)\,S(z) - L\bigl(\kappa(s)\bigr) - \log(2\pi), \qquad S(z) = \cos z - 1 = -2\sin^2\!\left(\frac{z}{2}\right), \qquad L(\kappa) = \log I_0(\kappa) - \kappa,
\f]
with \f$L(\kappa)\f$ evaluated by \ref laplace::math::own::log_bessel_i0_scaled "log_bessel_i0_scaled()" (\ref bessel_i "bessel_i"). Both \f$S(z)\f$ and \f$L(\kappa)\f$ are written so that nothing cancels at a small or a large concentration.

## Parameters, domain, and regularity

Struct `vonmises_distrib` sets `kind = distrib_kind::continuous`, `n_params = 2`, and `y_regularity = infinitely_smooth`. In `params`, `mu` has open domain \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ with `circular = true` (periodic of period \f$2\pi\f$) and `s` has open domain \f$(0, \infty)\f$ with `circular = false`; both set `moves_support = false`, `regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `cdf_regularity = infinitely_smooth`. The representative support runs from `support_lower()` (\f$-\pi\f$) to `support_upper()` (\f$\pi\f$), `center()` returns \f$\mu\f$ wrapped to \f$(-\pi, \pi]\f$, `scale()` returns \f$s\f$, and `param_kinks()` and `y_kinks()` return empty sets (`count = 0`). Wrapping \f$y - \mu\f$ returns quiet NaN when \f$|y - \mu| \ge 10^{15}\f$, and all evaluators return quiet NaN for \f$s \le 0\f$ or NaN inputs. Parameter derivatives pack into `sym_tensor<2, K>`, whose component \f$b\f$ holds \f$\partial^K \ell / \partial\mu^{K-b}\partial s^b\f$ (\ref distrib_core "distrib_core").

## Concentration map

Because \f$s(\kappa) = \sqrt{-2\log A(\kappa)}\f$ has no closed-form inverse, \ref laplace::distrib::vonmises_kappa_from_s "vonmises_kappa_from_s()" and \ref laplace::distrib::vonmises_kappa_from_s_dk "vonmises_kappa_from_s_dk()" split \f$(0, \infty)\f$ into three regimes at \f$s = 1/20\f$ (\f$0.05\f$) and \f$s = 4\f$, returning \f$+\infty\f$ for the value at \f$s = 0\f$ and quiet NaN outside \f$(0, \infty)\f$ for the derivatives:

- For \f$s < 0.05\f$, \f$\kappa\f$ diverges as \f$1/v\f$ with \f$v = s^2\f$ and only the complement \f$1 - \rho = -\operatorname{expm1}(-v/2)\f$ carries the information when \f$\rho\f$ rounds to \f$1\f$, so the map evaluates the \f$8\f$-term Laurent series \f$\kappa = 1/v + \sum_{j=0}^{7} c_j v^j\f$ (beginning \f$1/v + 1/2 + 5v/24\f$) and differentiates it term by term.
- For \f$0.05 \le s < 4\f$, the value calls \ref laplace::math::own::bessel_i_ratio_inv_c "bessel_i_ratio_inv_c()" on \f$q = -\operatorname{expm1}(-s^2/2)\f$ when \f$\rho > 0.5\f$ and \ref laplace::math::own::bessel_i_ratio_inv "bessel_i_ratio_inv()" on \f$\rho = e^{-s^2/2}\f$ otherwise (\ref bessel_i "bessel_i"), and derivatives follow by Faà di Bruno's formula (Johnson, 2002) composing the inverse derivatives \f$K^{(n)}\f$ at \f$\kappa\f$ with the derivatives of \f$\rho(s) = e^{-s^2/2}\f$.
- For \f$s \ge 4\f$, \f$\kappa \approx 2\rho\f$ and \f$\rho = e^{-s^2/2}\f$ underflows gracefully where \f$A^{-1}\f$ cannot resolve small \f$\rho\f$, so the map evaluates \f$\kappa = 2\rho + \rho^3 + \frac{5}{6}\rho^5 + \frac{19}{24}\rho^7\f$ and differentiates each \f$\rho^j = e^{-j s^2/2}\f$ term by term.

The coefficients of both series were obtained by reverting Taylor series from the Hankel asymptotic expansions (DLMF §10.40) and the power series at zero with SymPy (Meurer and others, 2017). Derivatives of the Gaussian factor \f$E(s) = e^{-a s^2/2}\f$ are generated by the recurrence
\f[
D_0 = e^{-a s^2/2}, \qquad D_1 = -a s D_0, \qquad D_{n+1} = -a(s D_n + n D_{n-1}), \qquad n \ge 1.
\f]
On the reference grid \f$s \in [10^{-8}, 4.5]\f$ (`tests/distrib/test_vonmises_scale.cpp`), the worst measured ulp errors on `gcc/OpenMP` (and test bounds for both `gcc/OpenMP` and `CUDA`) sit at \f$s = 4.1\f$ for order \f$0\f$ and at \f$s = 0.2\f$ for orders \f$1\f$ to \f$6\f$:

| Order | \f$0\f$ | \f$1\f$ | \f$2\f$ | \f$3\f$ | \f$4\f$ | \f$5\f$ | \f$6\f$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Measured max (ulp) | \f$5.32\f$ | \f$6.12\f$ | \f$9.55\f$ | \f$6.79\f$ | \f$20.4\f$ | \f$25.7\f$ | \f$25.3\f$ |
| Test bound (ulp) | \f$8\f$ | \f$10\f$ | \f$15\f$ | \f$20\f$ | \f$32\f$ | \f$40\f$ | \f$50\f$ |

Above \f$s = 4.5\f$, \f$\mathrm{d}\kappa/\kappa = -s^2\,\mathrm{d}s/s\f$, so a relative perturbation of \f$s\f$ of one unit in the last place moves \f$\kappa\f$ by \f$s^2\f$ units whatever computes it, setting the accuracy limit there. The joint routine \ref laplace::distrib::vonmises_kappa_from_s_upto "vonmises_kappa_from_s_upto()" returns \f$\kappa\f$ and derivatives \f$1\f$ to \f$N\f$ together, agrees with the per-order functions bit for bit, and at order \f$6\f$ on `gcc` takes \f$1302\f$ ns on one thread and \f$99\f$ ns on \f$16\f$ OpenMP threads against \f$4854\f$ ns and \f$367\f$ ns for six separate calls. It is an addition to the per-order functions and not a replacement for them: the saving is measured on this map, and a map elsewhere gets a joint form only once the same measurement is made for it.

## Derivatives and expected information

Because \f$\ell\f$ is a product in \f$s\f$ and \f$z\f$ minus a function of \f$s\f$, the component with \f$a\f$ derivatives in \f$\mu\f$ and \f$b\f$ in \f$s\f$ separates as
\f[
\frac{\partial^{a+b}\ell}{\partial\mu^a\partial s^b} = W_a(z)\,\kappa^{(b)}(s) - [a = 0]\frac{\mathrm{d}^b}{\mathrm{d}s^b}L\bigl(\kappa(s)\bigr),
\f]
where \f$[a = 0]\f$ equals \f$1\f$ when \f$a = 0\f$ and \f$0\f$ otherwise, and the six functions \f$W_a(z) = (-1)^a S^{(a)}(z)\f$ are \f$W_1(z) = \sin z\f$, \f$W_2(z) = -\cos z\f$, \f$W_3(z) = -\sin z\f$, \f$W_4(z) = \cos z\f$, \f$W_5(z) = \sin z\f$, and \f$W_6(z) = -\cos z\f$. Since \f$\mathrm{d}L/\mathrm{d}\kappa = A(\kappa) - 1 = -q\f$ with \f$q = -\operatorname{expm1}(-s^2/2)\f$, differentiating \f$\partial\ell/\partial s = \kappa'(s)(S(z) + q)\f$ by Leibniz's rule gives the pure-\f$s\f$ component of order \f$K \ge 1\f$ from \f$\kappa^{(1\dots K)}\f$ and the derivatives of \f$\rho = e^{-s^2/2}\f$ alone:
\f[
\frac{\partial^K\ell}{\partial s^K} = \kappa^{(K)}(s)\bigl(S(z) + q\bigr) - \sum_{j=1}^{K-1}\binom{K - 1}{j}\rho^{(j)}(s)\,\kappa^{(K-j)}(s).
\f]
Because \f$\partial/\partial y = -\partial/\partial\mu\f$, component \f$b\f$ of `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` (\f$J \ge 1\f$, \f$J + K \le 6\f$) is \f$(-1)^J W_{K-b+J}(z)\,\kappa^{(b)}(s)\f$.

The expected Fisher information is closed and diagonal:
\f[
I_{\mu\mu} = \kappa(s)\,\rho(s), \qquad I_{\mu s} = 0, \qquad I_{ss} = \frac{s^2\rho(s)^2}{A'\bigl(\kappa(s)\bigr)} = -s\,\rho(s)\,\kappa'(s).
\f]
The second form of \f$I_{ss}\f$, from \f$\rho'(s) = -s\rho(s) = A'(\kappa)\kappa'(s)\f$, is the one coded and needs no Bessel derivative; `expected_info_dk<K>()` (\f$K = 1, \dots, 4\f$) differentiates \f$I_{\mu\mu}\f$ and \f$I_{ss}\f$ in \f$s\f$ by Leibniz's rule while \f$\mu\f$-derivatives vanish. As \f$s \to 0\f$, \f$I_{ss} \sim 2/s^2\f$; as \f$s \to \infty\f$, \f$I_{ss} \sim 2 s^2\rho^2 \to 0\f$, because a uniform law does not identify \f$s\f$.

## Support, cumulative function, and generic routes

The representative interval is the fixed principal branch \f$[-\pi, \pi]\f$, so neither endpoint moves with a parameter (`cdf(y)` is \f$0\f$ for \f$y \le -\pi\f$ and \f$1\f$ for \f$y \ge \pi\f$). Measuring \f$F\f$ from the fixed lower end \f$-\pi\f$ gives
\f[
F(y) = \frac{y + \pi}{2\pi} + \frac{1}{\pi}\sum_{j=1}^{\infty} R_j(\kappa)\,\frac{\sin\bigl(j(y - \mu)\bigr) + \sin\bigl(j(\pi + \mu)\bigr)}{j}, \qquad R_j(\kappa) = \frac{I_j(\kappa)}{I_0(\kappa)},
\f]
where the second sine is the lower-limit term and does not vanish. This convention makes \f$F\f$ the cumulative distribution function of the representation on \f$[-\pi, \pi)\f$ rather than of the law on the circle: it is continuous, \f$2\pi\f$-periodic, and analytic in \f$\mu\f$, but it is not invariant under a rotation (\f$F(y; \mu) \ne F(y - c; \mu - c)\f$), and the cut sits in \f$y\f$ (\f$F(-\pi) = 0\f$, \f$F(\pi) = 1\f$). Censoring or truncating is meaningful only as a statement about an arc starting at \f$-\pi\f$. The size of that loss is of the order of \f$10^{-4}\f$: for a rotation \f$c = 0.4\f$, the difference \f$|F(y; \mu) - F(y - c; \mu - c)|\f$ reaches \f$4.0 \cdot 10^{-5}\f$ at \f$\mu = 0\f$ and \f$1.5 \cdot 10^{-4}\f$ at \f$\mu = 1\f$, measured with mpmath at 200 bits. A user who rotates the data must rotate the censoring bounds with them.

For \f$\kappa \le 3400\f$, `cdf()` evaluates \f$m = \min\bigl(512, \max\bigl(12, \lceil 8.6\sqrt{\kappa}\rceil + 10\bigr)\bigr)\f$ terms of the series via \ref laplace::math::own::bessel_i_ratios "bessel_i_ratios()" (\ref bessel_i "bessel_i") whenever \f$\min(F, 1 - F) \ge 10^{-3}\f$. Above \f$\kappa = 3400\f$ or below \f$10^{-3}\f$, \ref laplace::math::quad::panels_quad "panels_quad()" (\ref quadrature "quadrature") integrates the density over the arc (\f$[-\pi, y]\f$ or \f$[y, \pi]\f$) not holding the wrapped mode \f$\mu_w\f$, scaled by its larger endpoint value and panelled by the decay length \f$1/(\kappa|\sin(c - \mu_w)|)\f$ at that endpoint \f$c\f$ (floored at \f$1/\sqrt{\kappa}\f$). When the quadrature reports non-convergence, the arc mass on \f$(a, b)\f$ uses the change of variable \f$w = 2\sin((t - \mu_w)/2)\f$, giving exponent \f$-\kappa w^2/2\f$ and factor \f$g(w) = (1 - w^2/4)^{-1/2}\f$:
\f[
M \approx g(w^*)\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{\kappa}}\,\bigl[\Phi(-w_0\sqrt{\kappa}) - \Phi(-w_1\sqrt{\kappa})\bigr],
\f]
evaluated on the log scale via \ref laplace::math::own::log_Phi "log_Phi()" (\ref normal "normal"), where \f$w_0 \le w_1\f$ are the ordered endpoint magnitudes \f$|2\sin((a - \mu_w)/2)|\f$ and \f$|2\sin((b - \mu_w)/2)|\f$ and \f$w^*\f$ is \f$w_0\f$ moved inwards by one decay length (\f$1/(\kappa w_0)\f$ if \f$w_0\sqrt{\kappa} > 1\f$, else \f$1/\sqrt{\kappa}\f$, below \f$2\f$). The route is chosen by what the quadrature reports about itself rather than a threshold in \f$\kappa\f$, and the closed form's error falls like \f$1/\kappa\f$ where the quadrature degrades.

Quantiles, draws, and parameter derivatives of \f$\log F\f$ and \f$\log(1 - F)\f$ use \ref distrib_generic "the generic routes"; the generic log-cumulative derivative quadrature divides the integrand by \f$f(y)\f$ and may overflow when the cut separates two parts of a very concentrated law (\f$\mu\f$ within a few \f$s\f$ of \f$\pm\pi\f$). On the host, `start_value()` computes the circular moment estimate from the sample means \f$(c, \bar{s})\f$ of \f$(\cos y_i, \sin y_i)\f$, clamping \f$\hat s = \sqrt{-\log(c^2 + \bar{s}^2)}\f$ to \f$[10^{-4}, \sqrt{-\log 10^{-8}}]\f$ (\ref distrib_start "Starting values").

## Accuracy and speed

In `tests/distrib/test_vonmises.cpp`, log-density and mixed derivatives are checked inside Kokkos kernels against mpmath references in units of \f$\mathrm{ulp}(hi) + 4\varepsilon\,\mathrm{mag}\f$, where \f$hi\f$ is the reference value's high double, \f$\varepsilon\f$ is machine epsilon, and \f$\mathrm{mag}\f$ is the sum of term magnitudes (handling exact zeros where \f$\sin z = 0\f$, \f$\cos z = 0\f$, or \f$\cos z = \rho\f$). On `gcc`, measured maxima (and CUDA maxima of \f$4.70\f$ to \f$5.16\f$ for \f$s \le 4.5\f$ and \f$0.10\f$ to \f$0.81\f$ for \f$s > 4.5\f$) stay within the test bounds \f$8\f$ (\f$s \le 4.5\f$) and \f$8(1 + 0.25 s^2)\f$ (\f$s > 4.5\f$):

| Quantity | \f$0\f$ | \f$1\f$ | \f$2\f$ | \f$3\f$ | \f$4\f$ | \f$5\f$ | \f$6\f$ | Bound |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| \f$\log f\f$, \f$s \le 4.5\f$ | \f$4.70\f$ | \f$5.01\f$ | \f$4.99\f$ | \f$5.10\f$ | \f$4.87\f$ | \f$4.90\f$ | \f$5.02\f$ | \f$8\f$ |
| \f$\log f\f$, \f$s > 4.5\f$ | \f$0.10\f$ | \f$0.53\f$ | \f$0.49\f$ | \f$0.53\f$ | \f$0.55\f$ | \f$0.61\f$ | \f$0.81\f$ | \f$8(1 + 0.25 s^2)\f$ |
| Mixed \f$(y, \theta)\f$, \f$s \le 4.5\f$ | — | \f$0.52\f$ | \f$0.70\f$ | \f$1.15\f$ | \f$1.30\f$ | \f$2.50\f$ | \f$3.33\f$ | \f$8\f$ |
| Mixed \f$(y, \theta)\f$, \f$s > 4.5\f$ | — | \f$0.53\f$ | \f$0.49\f$ | \f$0.53\f$ | \f$0.55\f$ | \f$0.57\f$ | \f$0.60\f$ | \f$8(1 + 0.25 s^2)\f$ |

For the cumulative functions (`cdf()`, `ccdf()`, `log_cdf()`, `log_ccdf()`), the worst error against mpmath quadrature reaches \f$0.24\f$ of the test tolerance on the series route and \f$0.18\f$ on the tail integral, the tolerance being a few units in the last place of the probability itself. Where \f$w_0\sqrt{\kappa}\f$ is of order one (checked on \f$0.2 < |2\sin((y-\mu)/2)|\sqrt{\kappa} < 5\f$), the two arc-mass routes meet and \f$F\f$ is accurate to about \f$2.9 \times 10^{-10}\f$ relative (\f$1.8 \times 10^{-9}\f$ on \f$\log F\f$) instead of the \f$10^{-15}\f$ reached elsewhere. The expected information and its derivatives are checked against mpmath quadratures of their definitions. See `docs/numerics.md` for the full measurements.

On `gcc`, `logpdf()` takes \f$622\f$ ns on one thread and \f$47\f$ ns with OpenMP, the order-\f$6\f$ tensor takes \f$2891\f$ ns on one thread and \f$204\f$ ns with OpenMP of which the map alone is \f$2746\f$ ns and \f$200\f$ ns so above order three the cost of a derivative tensor is the cost of the map, `expected_info()` takes \f$821\f$ ns and \f$67\f$ ns, `cdf()` takes \f$29613\f$ ns and \f$1643\f$ ns, and `rng_by_inversion()` takes \f$72074\f$ ns and \f$5851\f$ ns.

## Kokkos usage

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/vonmises.hpp>

void eval_vonmises(Kokkos::View<const double*> y, Kokkos::View<double*> out, double mu, double s) {
  const laplace::distrib::vonmises_distrib d{mu, s};
  Kokkos::parallel_for("vonmises_eval", y.extent(0), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    out(i) = d.logpdf(y(i)) + d.logpdf_d1(y(i))[0];
  });
}
```

## Members

Struct \ref laplace::distrib::vonmises_distrib "vonmises_distrib" implements \ref distrib_core "distrib_core" for `n_params = 2`: `mu`, `s`, `kind`, `n_params`, `y_regularity`, `params`, `logpdf()`, `logpdf_d1()`..`logpdf_d6()`, `logpdf_dk()`, `logpdf_dy_dk()`, `expected_info()`, `expected_info_d1()`..`expected_info_d4()`, `expected_info_dk()`, `cdf()`, `ccdf()`, `log_cdf()`, `log_ccdf()`, `support_lower()`, `support_upper()`, `center()`, `scale()`, `param_kinks()`, `y_kinks()`, and `start_value()`. The map functions in `laplace::distrib` are \ref laplace::distrib::vonmises_kappa_from_s "vonmises_kappa_from_s()", \ref laplace::distrib::vonmises_kappa_from_s_dk "vonmises_kappa_from_s_dk()", and \ref laplace::distrib::vonmises_kappa_from_s_upto "vonmises_kappa_from_s_upto()".

